.TH BACKLIGHT 1 "September 2026" "LikeOS" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
backlight \- read and set the display backlight
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B backlight
.RB [ \-d
.IR device ]
.RB [ \-q ]
.br
.B backlight
.RB [ \-d
.IR device ]
.I level
|
.IB n %
|
.BI + n %
|
.BI \- n %
|
.B on
|
.B off
.br
.B backlight \-l
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B backlight
prints or changes the brightness of a display backlight the kernel
drives.  Each such backlight is a directory under
.IR /sys/class/backlight/ ,
with the files
.IR brightness ,
.IR max_brightness ,
.I actual_brightness
and
.IR bl_power ;
.B backlight
is a convenience over reading and writing them.
.PP
Without an argument the current level, the maximum and the percentage
are printed.  A plain number sets the level in the device's own units
(0 to
.IR max_brightness );
a number followed by
.B %
sets it as a percentage of the maximum; with a leading
.B +
or
.B \-
the value is relative to the current one.
.B off
switches the backlight off (the panel keeps its picture, dark) and
.B on
switches it back on.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-d " device"
The device under
.I /sys/class/backlight/
to use.  The default is the first one there; on a machine with Intel
graphics that is
.IR intel_backlight .
.TP
.B \-l
List the backlight devices with their type and level.
.TP
.B \-q
Print the level only, as a number.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B backlight 50%
Half brightness.
.TP
.B backlight +10%
A step up.
.TP
.B backlight off
Dark, for example while a long download runs.
.SH FILES
.I /sys/class/backlight/*/brightness
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR drminfo (1),
.BR xrandr (1)
